---
title: Real Youth Employment
order: 2
icon: briefcase
---

Nairobi's youth unemployment rate is a time bomb. Hundreds of thousands of young Kenyans — many with diplomas, degrees, and certificates — roam the city daily in search of work that does not exist. The informal sector, or *jua kali*, absorbs many, but it offers no security, no benefits, and no path to a dignified livelihood. The county government has treated youth employment as an afterthought, offering sporadic internships and empty promises while the crisis deepens.

We demand a comprehensive youth employment strategy rooted in partnership with Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) institutions. The county must establish a Nairobi Youth Skills Fund that co-finances industry-aligned training programs in construction, digital technology, renewable energy, hospitality, and the creative economy. Every training program must culminate in a certified apprenticeship of no less than six months with a registered employer, with the county covering 50 percent of the apprentice's stipend.

For young entrepreneurs, we demand a one-stop business registration and licensing center that issues permits within 48 hours, a county-level startup grant program for businesses in the green economy and technology sectors, and access to affordable workspace in every sub-county. We will establish a Nairobi Enterprise Board that provides mentorship, market linkages, and low-interest credit to youth-led enterprises. Casual labor contracts — the *kazi kwa kazi* exploitation model — must be phased out in favor of formal, benefits-protected employment.

Every shilling spent on youth programs must be tracked and published. We will create an online Youth Employment Dashboard showing real-time data on training placements, apprenticeship uptake, startup funding disbursed, and jobs created. The young people of Nairobi are not looking for handouts — they are demanding a government that takes their future as seriously as they do.
